1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,600
Struggling to pick a subject for uni?

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,700
No idea which way to go for an apprenticeship?

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:08,100
Maybe you've just got…

4
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:09,500
an inkling?

5
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:12,000
Inkling uncovers the subjects that fit you.

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
From what you're actually into.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,800
Backed by real data.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,800
Your top three subjects.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,200
Plus a wildcard.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,400
Hi, I'm Martin.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,200
I build university websites. I see this every day.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
So for this hackathon, from an empty repo, I built Inkling.

13
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,300
Let me show you.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
An inkling is that feeling you sort of know what you'd like to do, but you're not sure.

15
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,500
Inkling helps you confirm it.

16
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:42,800
Four quick rounds.

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,900
First, tap the subjects you take.

18
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,200
If one's missing, start typing and pick it from the list.

19
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,500
Then swipe through things you might get stuck into, pick between pictures, and answer a few big questions.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Everything you choose lands in your room.

21
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,500
At the end, you get your top three subjects, based on everything you picked.

22
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Tap any object to see which subject it stands for.

23
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:20,200
And there's a wildcard: one that fits your answers, but you'd never have gone looking for.

24
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,500
Then it writes your inkling, live. Muse Spark reads your answers and says why your top subject fits.

25
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,800
And Muse Image draws your paper pal while you wait. Both are live Meta model calls, every game.

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,500
Each subject opens real UK courses, by city, coast or campus town, and apprenticeships if you'd rather earn while you learn.

27
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,500
And it makes a ten-second video of your room to share.

28
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Meta's models did two jobs here. They built the game, and they run live inside it.

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,500
Before launch, Muse Spark Open on Baseten wrote all 163 subject cards, then reviewed its own work.

30
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:02,300
It rated five thousand course titles, tagged every campus and linked the apprenticeships.

31
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,500
Muse Image drew the whole paper world.

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,500
SAM 3.1 checks every sticker. And Muse Code helped me build it.

33
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:13,800
Because it's open weights, a school could run all of this on its own servers.

34
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:23,500
Before any real students played, Spark Open wrote 200 simulated students, each with the subjects they'd suit noted first, and played every round as them.

35
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,500
That showed where the scoring needed work: Zoology was landing in 45 students' top three when only one suited it.

36
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:35,500
After the fix, on students it had never seen, a fitting subject in the top three went from 38 to 54 percent.

37
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,800
Now real players can say if it felt right.

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
And you can run a new student any time: Spark Open writes one live on Baseten, plays every round, and the real scoring code walks them to their building.

39
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Everything you saw was built this week, on real data from HESA, O*NET and Skills England.

40
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Free, no sign-up, any phone.

41
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:57,000
Find your inkling.
